1
00:00:02,650 --> 00:00:06,370
Every morning, a mirror shows you who you are.

2
00:00:10,650 --> 00:00:14,275
This one showed Theo who he was going to be.

3
00:00:14,650 --> 00:00:16,052
THEO: Huh.

4
00:00:18,250 --> 00:00:20,336
THEO: Oh. Hi?

5
00:00:21,650 --> 00:00:26,698
Theo was twenty-four. The man in the glass was not.

6
00:00:38,850 --> 00:00:42,519
He did what anyone would do. He tested it.

7
00:00:42,650 --> 00:00:44,385
THEO: Left hand.

8
00:00:45,850 --> 00:00:54,050
Fun fact: mirrors don’t really swap left and right. They swap front and back. That’s why the words on your T-shirt come out backwards.

9
00:00:54,050 --> 00:00:57,650
And the face in a mirror is always a little out of date.

10
00:00:57,650 --> 00:01:04,050
Stand a metre from the glass, and the light takes about seven billionths of a second to come back to you.

11
00:01:04,050 --> 00:01:07,850
Theo’s mirror was running about ten years ahead.

12
00:01:07,850 --> 00:01:13,250
He studied the man. Same nose. Same hair, less of it. Tired eyes.

13
00:01:13,250 --> 00:01:18,954
An office badge. And in the corner, his cello. Shut, and dusty.

14
00:01:20,650 --> 00:01:23,172
THEO: Okay. Phone camera.

15
00:01:24,050 --> 00:01:25,552
THEO: Normal.

16
00:01:28,250 --> 00:01:33,188
The mirror in the hallway was normal too. Only the bathroom one knew.

17
00:01:40,450 --> 00:01:43,831
THEO: Nan, does your mirror ever show you older?

18
00:01:45,050 --> 00:01:49,281
NAN: Every morning, love. It’s called being seventy-eight.

19
00:01:50,450 --> 00:01:53,997
THEO: No, a lot older. Like, ten years.

20
00:01:54,450 --> 00:01:57,154
NAN: Ah. And what’s he like?

21
00:01:57,850 --> 00:02:00,801
THEO: Tired. Like he gave up on something.

22
00:02:02,050 --> 00:02:05,848
NAN: Then he’s a stranger. You haven’t met him yet.

23
00:02:07,450 --> 00:02:13,250
Psychologists asked over nineteen thousand people about their personalities.

24
00:02:13,250 --> 00:02:20,250
They expected to change very little in the next ten years. But they said they’d changed a lot in the last ten.

25
00:02:20,250 --> 00:02:25,628
We always think we’re nearly finished. It’s called the end of history illusion.

26
00:02:31,250 --> 00:02:35,448
So Theo started running experiments.

27
00:02:38,250 --> 00:02:42,328
He walked to work instead of taking the bus.

28
00:02:56,250 --> 00:03:01,186
The next morning, the man in the glass stood a little straighter.

29
00:03:02,650 --> 00:03:08,019
He opened the cello. Ten minutes a night. His neighbours were very patient.

30
00:03:21,050 --> 00:03:25,304
In the glass, the cello case was open.

31
00:03:25,550 --> 00:03:27,292
THEO: Did you see that?

32
00:03:32,650 --> 00:03:41,886
Here’s something odd about brains. When people imagine themselves in ten years, their brains respond a bit like they’re thinking about someone else.

33
00:03:42,050 --> 00:03:46,850
In one experiment, people met an aged picture of themselves first.

34
00:03:46,850 --> 00:03:51,570
Then they set aside more than twice as much for retirement.

35
00:03:51,850 --> 00:03:55,919
Theo had the full-size version. Every morning.

36
00:04:00,650 --> 00:04:06,246
New habits take about sixty-six days, on average, to become automatic.

37
00:04:06,250 --> 00:04:10,165
Some took eighteen. Some took over two hundred.

38
00:04:22,250 --> 00:04:27,164
On day sixty-six, he took the cello up to the roof.

39
00:04:48,650 --> 00:04:51,420
NAN: Not bad at all, love!

40
00:04:51,650 --> 00:04:53,469
THEO: Thank you, Nan!

41
00:05:08,650 --> 00:05:13,716
On day seventy, the mirror showed the bathroom. Just the bathroom.

42
00:05:14,250 --> 00:05:15,734
THEO: Hello?

43
00:05:17,050 --> 00:05:18,633
THEO: Where are you?

44
00:05:20,450 --> 00:05:26,159
No man. No badge. No cello. Ten years from now, nobody lived here.

45
00:05:30,650 --> 00:05:33,988
THEO: He’s gone. The one in the mirror is gone.

46
00:05:34,850 --> 00:05:37,137
NAN: Where did he go?

47
00:05:37,850 --> 00:05:40,304
THEO: I don’t know. Nowhere.

48
00:05:41,250 --> 00:05:44,671
NAN: Or somewhere else. Did you look properly?

49
00:05:50,650 --> 00:05:54,522
He hadn’t vanished. He’d moved out.

50
00:05:58,850 --> 00:06:00,518
THEO: Port Alder.

51
00:06:02,250 --> 00:06:08,437
Bodies are always moving out, too. Your red blood cells only last about four months.

52
00:06:08,650 --> 00:06:15,214
But most of the neurons in the outer layer of your brain are as old as you are. Some of you stays.

53
00:06:34,250 --> 00:06:39,422
Port Alder had an orchestra. The orchestra had auditions.

54
00:06:39,650 --> 00:06:41,886
THEO: Okay. Okay.

55
00:07:01,050 --> 00:07:04,779
On his last morning, Theo didn’t check the mirror.

56
00:07:04,850 --> 00:07:10,249
He’d seen enough to start. The rest, he wanted to find out by getting there.

57
00:07:13,050 --> 00:07:16,122
NAN: You send me a postcard, love.

58
00:07:16,450 --> 00:07:18,521
THEO: I think I already did.

59
00:07:46,650 --> 00:07:48,968
PANEL: Whenever you’re ready.

60
00:08:02,050 --> 00:08:04,749
PANEL: Thank you. We’ll be in touch.

61
00:08:06,450 --> 00:08:08,889
And they were.

62
00:08:26,650 --> 00:08:32,224
Ten years later, in a flat by the sea, a man stepped up to a mirror.

63
00:08:32,250 --> 00:08:35,791
It showed him at forty-four.

64
00:08:37,850 --> 00:08:39,380
THEO: Hi, you.

65
00:08:42,050 --> 00:08:47,913
He didn’t ask what he’d become. He knew the trick now. It gets decided every morning.
